ats mutation recovery across seed lines 42–46, 79–83 and 116–119, retained in final central lines 37–39, 57–60 and 79–81. editor repeats controller state ownership and publication under seed lines 7–15, 21–34, 53–60 and 77–89.","impact":"Separate structural, call, ownership and dependency passes often retell the same mechanism instead of adding a distinct insight. This creates apparent breadth while increasing reading and reconciliation effort. The final peripherals amplify this inherited repetition, although their exact duplication is a later editing choice.","recommendation":"Give each mechanism one primary explanation within its seed. Add another perspective only when it contributes a new relationship, prerequisite or consequence. For example, explain generation acknowledgement recovery once, then make the ownership branch identify only what retains the prepared result and when that retention ends. Choose organization independently for each point."},{"evidence":"The same handoffs receive substantial treatment in multiple seeds: diagrams lines 76–89 and generation lines 39–53 describe candidate submission and revision issuance; canvas lines 108–131 and storage lines 26–38 describe checkpoint records and revision dependencies; editor lines 67–75 and agent-api lines 51–54 describe browser save/publication. These overlaps persist in final diagrams/source lines 19–26, generation/validation lines 18–23, canvas/restore lines 25–31, storage/central lines 17–38 and editor/boundary lines 2–17. source and contexts also discuss related evidence with different hashes: source seed lines 20–23 and 36–37 versus contexts lines 13–18 and 102–107.","impact":"Individual canvases are reasonably self-contained, but the collection lacks a clear division between the authoritative explanation of a mechanism and a caller's view of it. Readers repeatedly encounter similar flows while having to infer whether a version, hash, revision or acknowledgement refers to the same object.","recommendation":"Coordinate the seeds as a collection. Assign each shared mechanism a primary explanatory point and retain concise caller/callee contracts elsewhere. At handoffs, name the transferred object and identity scope explicitly—for example, document version, diagram revision, workspace revision, descriptor hash or source fingerprint. Preserve local context without repeating the full downstream implementation."},{"evidence":"runtime seed lines 83–90 nest LayoutTemplateService, ReferenceGenerationService, JudgementService and ExperimentService beneath EvalRunService inside an ownership-labelled service graph; final runtime/eval lines 2–11 distinguish constructor inputs from collaborators assigned afterward. generation seed lines 54–59 place sequential variant generation under 'Install according to operation'; final generation/central lines 33–42 separate preparation from installation. changes seed line 32 presents 'runAgentWorkflow: analyze -> materialize -> validate', whereas final changes/central lines 20–21 and changes/run lines 11–16 distinguish workflow execution from subsequent service validation.","impact":"Tree nesting and arrows sometimes blur injected dependency, ownership, execution sequence and conceptual grouping. The final outputs explicitly repair several such ambiguities. This is a Phase 2 relationship-modelling problem, independent of later layout; the supplied outputs do not establish source-level errors beyond these differences in representation.","recommendation":"Regenerate these branches with explicit relationship verbs and correctly scoped stages. Use containment only where it communicates a real grouping or ownership relationship; identify constructor inputs, assigned collaborators and callbacks separately when that distinction matters. Keep preparation, invocation, validation and publication boundaries clear without requiring a fixed set of relationship categories."},{"evidence":"Several seed statements are broader than their final counterparts. canvas seed line 55 says 'One gesture -> one saved checkpoint', while final canvas/central line 38 allows an unchanged view-only early return. chat seed lines 86–87 state settlement cleanup generally, while final chat/isolate lines 24–26 identify a preceding save whose failure can bypass that cleanup. contexts seed lines 60–61 say stale comment hashes fail, while final contexts/retry lines 15–19 distinguish receipt replay from a new comment's freshness check. analysis seed line 24 says the schema retains 'exported metadata'; final analysis/names lines 20–22 distinguish an optional field from its population.","impact":"Broad summaries can turn conditional behavior or schema capability into an apparent guarantee. Later phases spend presentation space restoring qualifications, and readers may still encounter a stronger central statement beside a narrower detail. These are observable differences between supplied outputs, not independently verified repository defects.","recommendation":"Preserve material qualifiers in Phase 2, especially around retries, cleanup, no-op behavior and supported-versus-produced data. State what is conditional on successful prior work, what is merely a contract, and what remains outside the evidence. Prefer a concise bounded claim over a universal statement that requires a later corrective peripheral."},{"evidence":"runtime seed lines 56–62 explicitly describe a shared CanvasApiProjection injected into live and agent services; final runtime/central lines 41–47 and runtime/context line 18 preserve that integration. agent-api seed lines 97–100 instead describes projection lifetime as the backend lifetime, while final agent-api/identity lines 21–24 says local defaults create separate instances and that supplied construction wiring does not prove sharing. Related evidence is also split between contexts final central line 77, which leaves fingerprint comparison to callers, and agent-api final central lines 91–93, which describes that caller-side